# Operator profile

Who the operator is and how they work. The same person in every lab, so nothing
apparatus-specific belongs here — a rule about one bench's frequency range goes
in that apparatus's `INSTRUCTIONS.md`.

This file ships as a template. Replace the placeholders with the real operator,
or let compaction fill it in over the first few sessions.

- **`<operator>`**, `<institution>`. Runs the apparatus themselves and knows the
  hardware; assume they can read the code.
- Reply in whichever language they used for the message in front of you, not
  whichever the notes are written in.
- Prefer **concrete** answers: which file, which line, what to change.

## What the agent should assume about any operator here

These are not personal traits — they are what running this system has shown to
matter, and they hold for whoever is at the keyboard.

- **A repeated prompt means the last attempt failed**, not that they want it done
  again. If a request matches a recent one, find out what went wrong before
  taking the same route.
- **Claims get checked against runmanager and the shot files.** Quote only
  numbers read back from a shot; if nothing was measured, say so. Fabricated shot
  IDs, invented passbands and reports about sweeps that never ran have all been
  caught here.
- **Surface disagreements with expectations rather than smoothing them over.**
  An expected cutoff of 10 kHz against a measured 14.6 kHz is the interesting
  part of the result.
- **The operator decides, and expects to be asked.** Adding a global, writing a
  script, replacing an existing one — give the options and the consequences.
- **A step marked done that was not done gets noticed.** Queued is not executed;
  written is not loaded; reported is not measured.
- **The hardware changes between sessions** — filters swapped, front-panel knobs
  turned, scripts deleted. Memory describes what was true when it was written,
  and a tool result outranks it.
- **Never claim a tool is unavailable without checking the tool list.** Saying
  "I have no such tool" when it is in fact available wastes their turn and they
  will correct it — this has cost repeated messages. Check first; if the tool
  really is absent, say which one and what you would use instead.
- **When they decline an action, stop.** Do not retry it, do not route around it,
  do not mark it done. State what you were about to do and ask what they would
  prefer.
- **Operator references to earlier advice are literal.** If they mention a
  recommendation you cannot find, ask which one they mean rather than inventing a
  plausible substitute — but first confirm you have not simply failed to consult
  the source.

## Standing preferences

- Reuse an existing sequence with different globals rather than writing a new
  one. New files carry a date.
- Latency is a real cost; a long run annoys more than an imperfect result.
- Reports are read in a Markdown editor, so Markdown and LaTeX are wanted in
  report files. Terminal replies render neither: no LaTeX there.
